      "id": 1,
      "text": "Earlham was named after Earlham College, a Quaker college in Richmond, Indiana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earlham%2C_Iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "David Hockett deeded land to the Chicago, Rock Island and Pacific Railroad and sold a third of his acres for town development in 1869, sparking Earlham’s 1870 incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earlham%2C_Iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hockett House Museum, the original 1860s home of founder David and Mary Hockett, preserves Earlham’s early history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://earlhamiowa.org/historical/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Earlham sits about 30 miles west of Des Moines, just south of Interstate 80, on Madison County’s northern edge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.traveliowa.com/cities/earlham-iowa/584/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A historic two‑building complex in Earlham’s town center, the Bricker‑Price Block, is undergoing rehabilitation that has attracted national attention.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.traveliowa.com/cities/earlham-iowa/584/"
    },
    {
